                    <fr:link href="/dhsorens/" title="Derek Sorensen" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens/" display-uri="dhsorens" type="local">Derek Sorensen</fr:link>
                  </fr:author>
                </fr:authors>
                <fr:date>
                  <fr:year>2026</fr:year>
                  <fr:month>8</fr:month>
                  <fr:day>18</fr:day>
                </fr:date>
                <fr:uri>https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-003L/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>dhsorens-003L</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/dhsorens-003L/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="IVC and Proof-Carrying Data">IVC and Proof-Carrying Data</fr:title>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    The definitions that folding and recursion are trying to achieve, and the overhead problem that shapes every construction: the verifier has to be expressible as a circuit in the system it verifies.
</html:p>
                <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>Incrementally verifiable computation and proof-carrying data: the statements, and <fr:link href="/dhsorens-004J/" title="Soundness, Knowledge Soundness, and Extraction" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-004J/" display-uri="dhsorens-004J" type="local">the knowledge soundness they require</fr:link>.</html:li>
        <html:li>Recursion overhead: what a verifier-in-circuit costs, and which components dominate (hashing, MSM, field arithmetic).</html:li>
        <html:li>Cycles of curves, and why hash-based systems avoid the problem differently.</html:li>
        <html:li><fr:link href="/dhsorens-004N/" title="From Soundness Error to Bits of Security" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-004N/" display-uri="dhsorens-004N" type="local">How the security loss compounds over recursion depth</fr:link>.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
