            <fr:title text="Memory Checking in zkVMs">Memory Checking in zkVMs</fr:title>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>
    Random-access memory is the hardest part of a zkVM to constrain and the most common source of unsoundness. The general machinery is in <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002X/" title="Permutation, Grand-Product, and Memory Arguments" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002X/" display-uri="dhsorens-002X" type="local">permutation and memory arguments</fr:link>; this page is about what the systems actually do.
</html:p>
            <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>Read-write RAM by offline memory checking, as in SP1, Jolt, and RISC Zero.</html:li>
        <html:li>Paged memory with Merkle verification, which RISC Zero also supports, and when that is preferable.</html:li>
        <html:li>Stack-based models (Miden, zkWasm) and how their consistency arguments differ.</html:li>
        <html:li>Memory across <fr:link href="/dhsorens-003O/" title="Continuations and Sharding" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-003O/" display-uri="dhsorens-003O" type="local">shard boundaries</fr:link> — the argument that ties independent segments together.</html:li>
        <html:li>The concrete cost of a load or store, counted in trace cells and lookups.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
            <html:p>
    Sources: <fr:link href="https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/525" type="external">SoK: Understanding zkVM</fr:link>, <fr:link href="https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/1605" type="external">Nebula</fr:link>.
</html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
