            <fr:title text="Performance and Benchmarking">Performance and Benchmarking</fr:title>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>
    What actually costs time, and how to read a published comparison. The target is a cost model good enough to predict the direction of a change before measuring it.
</html:p>
            <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>The cost breakdown: witness generation, hashing, FFTs, MSMs, extension-field arithmetic.</html:li>
        <html:li>Cycles per second as a metric, and what it hides — precompiles, <fr:link href="/dhsorens-003O/" title="Continuations and Sharding" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-003O/" display-uri="dhsorens-003O" type="local">shard</fr:link> size, security parameters.</html:li>
        <html:li>Proof size and verifier time across systems, and the recursion that produces the final numbers.</html:li>
        <html:li>How the <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002K/" title="Finite Fields and the Small-Field Turn" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002K/" display-uri="dhsorens-002K" type="local">field choice</fr:link> shows up in measurements, and why hash rate is often the real headline.</html:li>
        <html:li>Comparing at equal security: the benchmark question that is usually skipped.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
            <html:p>
    Sources: <fr:link href="https://github.com/a16z/zkvm-benchmarks" type="external">zkvm-benchmarks</fr:link>.
</html:p>
